CS3365 – Group Project: Phase 2
Phase 2 of the project is based on the "Lottery Purchase System (LPS)" description provided in
Phase 1. After reviewing that description again, complete the following tasks with your team
members.
1. (25 points) Develop a class diagram for the "Lottery Purchase System (LPS)." Even if you
plan to develop the software without using classes, imagine this as a real-world project that
requires multiple users, each with their own instance/object in the system. Therefore, approach
this task as if you are building the system using classes. (Use computer tools like PowerPoint,
Word, or other computer-aided tools to create these diagrams. Don’t include hand-drawn
diagrams). When creating this diagram, you must use correct UML notations, follow best
practices, and apply the clarity techniques covered in class.
2. (25 points) State diagrams illustrate how a single object behaves in response to a series of
events within your system. Choose the two most active objects from your system and create
separate state diagrams for each. (Similar to the exercises we did in class).
Name of the first object: ____________
• Draw the first state diagram.
Name of the second object: ____________
• Draw the second state diagram.
When creating these diagrams, you must use correct UML notations, follow best practices, and
apply the clarity techniques covered in class.
3. (45 points) Develop a working program for the description given in phase 1. This program
must have a graphical interface. Although you were given more than a month to complete it, do
not wait until the last week to start. Whether you choose to develop this project as a website,
mobile app, or graphical Java application, ect, keep in mind that research and implementation
take significant time. Starting late will jeopardize your ability to produce a high-quality final
result.
Please note that this is a team project, and you do not have to develop this program by yourself.
Once you are done developing this program, create a video describing each functionality of the
software. This is part of the project presentation, and each member of your team needs to
participate in the presentation. (You do not need to involve any non-participating team members
in the presentation.)
In the program, demonstrate each implemented functionality, such as a user browsing and
purchasing tickets. For example, after logging out and back in, the user should be able to view all
previously purchased tickets, etc. In the video, you need to show each functionality in action
(not just pointing out it’s there. You need to show how the process works). Attempting to
mislead the instructor by using fake or static code will result in a zero grade for the entire
project.
When submitting Phase 2, your video needs to showcase how to operate the program. Ensure
that all stakeholder-requested functionalities are demonstrated in the video. Failing to
demonstrate all functionalities in the video will result in a point deduction. You do not need to
describe the code, as I will review it manually.
4. (5 points) During your design and development of the project, consider possible local and
global impacts of the project on individuals, organizations, and society. (We will talk about this
topic in a future lecture). Once you analyze these impacts, describe your thoughts.
5. (Required) Document the work completed by each team member. All team members must
contribute equally and support each other to ensure a successful project. If any issues arise in a
section developed by another member, work together to resolve them.
